# cb-vpn destination deny-list (tinyproxy Filter, FilterExtended On).
#
# One POSIX extended regex per line, matched case-insensitively against the
# destination host of every request and CONNECT.
#
# This is the *first* of two layers. It catches the literal cases and gives a
# clean 403 instead of a hang, but it cannot see where a hostname resolves — a
# name that answers 169.254.169.254 sails straight through. The DOCKER-USER
# iptables rules (firewall.sh) are the layer that actually holds, because they
# filter on the resolved destination address. Neither layer alone is sufficient.

# Loopback: 127.0.0.0/8 in dotted form, plus the name.
^127\.
^localhost$
\.localdomain$

# 0.0.0.0/8 — "this network". 0.0.0.0 is a routable alias for loopback on Linux,
# and leading-zero octets (0177.0.0.1) are parsed as octal by some resolvers.
^0\.
^0+$

# Bare integers: http://2130706433/ is 127.0.0.1 to inet_addr(). Any host that is
# all digits is an encoded address, never a real name.
^[0-9]+$

# RFC1918.
^10\.
^172\.(1[6-9]|2[0-9]|3[01])\.
^192\.168\.

# Link-local, including 169.254.169.254 cloud metadata.
^169\.254\.

# Cloud metadata by name — the addresses above are the real target, but the
# hostnames are what an SSRF payload usually carries.
^metadata\.google\.internal$
^metadata\.goog$
^instance-data(\.|$)

# Tailnet (CGNAT 100.64.0.0/10). This is how the proxy would reach the VPS itself
# and every other machine on the user's tailnet.
^100\.(6[4-9]|[7-9][0-9]|1[01][0-9]|12[0-7])\.

# IPv6 loopback and unique-local (fc00::/7 = fc.. and fd..), with or without the
# brackets a URL authority puts around them. Link-local fe80::/10 too.
^\[?::1\]?$
^\[?::\]?$
^\[?f[cd][0-9a-f]{2}:
^\[?fe[89ab][0-9a-f]:
